From a7ef13d9e0277a76290da5ad896c7228a3a5f2cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Klemens Arro <1125090+kempu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:59:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] directory: build an OIDC directory whose identity
 provider is down, and retry discovery on use

OpenIdDirectory::new fetched the discovery document and the JWKS once.
When either failed (the identity provider was down, or started after
this server), open() returned an error, the directory was recorded as a
build error and left out, and nothing ever tried again. Every Bearer
token then fell through to the internal token decoder and was rejected
as "Failed to decode token" until a restart. And with the directory
missing, the default directory was missing too, so a Basic login went to
the internal directory instead of the identity provider.

The directory is now always built. Discovery is attempted when it is
built and, when that fails, again when a token arrives: at most once per
backoff period (10 s after the first failure, doubling to 30 s), by one
caller at a time. A caller that arrives while an attempt is in flight
does not wait for it. Until discovery succeeds every token presented to
the directory is rejected as an authentication error ("Identity provider
unavailable"): nothing is verified without the provider's keys, and an
error is not a failed login, so it does not count towards an address's
authentication ban. Once discovery succeeds the directory behaves as
before: the issuer, audience and scope checks, the JWKS cache and its
refresh on an unknown key id are unchanged.

A failed attempt logs auth.warning with its cause and when the next one
is due; the first success after failures logs resource.download-external
(info: the only info-level auth event is auth.success). A discovery
document for another issuer is a misconfiguration, not an outage: it is
logged as auth.error and still retried, so fixing the provider does not
need a restart here.

/api/discover answers 503 for a domain whose identity provider has not
been discovered, instead of this server's own metadata, which would send
the client to the wrong issuer. The PACC record names the configured
issuer URL, which needs no discovery.

The tests run a stand-in provider on 127.0.0.1 that serves a JWKS with
an RSA key made for the test (aws-lc-rs, already in the lock file, is a
new dev-dependency). Upstream's own OIDC integration test (tests/, under
the Stalwart Enterprise License, not built here) edits the discovery
document through a field that no longer exists.
